I prepared a small speech for you guys Um I'm not a public speaker so I apologize for the muttering Glad you're here Um ladies and gentlemen council members and fellow citizens the United States of America was founded by men who understood something deeply about human nature and power They knew that liberty is rarely lost all at once More often it disappears slowly through small concessions quiet expansions of authority and policies justified in the name of safety convenience or efficiency Here in Wyoming that principle carries special meaning The state was built by people who valued independence personal responsibility and local control over distant authority Wyomingites have never been comfortable with power that grows quietly without clear consent of the people Benjamin Franklin warned us plainly "Those who would give up essential liberty to purchase a little temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety." Franklin wrote these words as a warning because he knew that governments even well-intentioned ones are always tempted to expand their reach George Washington reminded the young nation "Government is not reason It is not eloquence It is force like fire It is dangerous servant

and a fearful master." These words are not anti-government They are pro-liberty They remind us that government must always remain accountable to the people who created it And that principle matters today right here in our own communities When citizens see expanding surveillance through systems like flock cameras when they see administrative warrants that bypass traditional judicial oversight and when they see communities facing forced annexation without the clear consent of the people that live there people begin to ask a simple question Who is our government serving Is the people is it serving the people who elected it or is it serving the convenience of government itself Thomas Jefferson reminded us the price of liberty is eternal vigilance Vigilance means asking hard questions It means expecting elected officials to vote in the interest of their constituents not in the interest of political comfort administrative convenience or personal advantage And

when elected officials forget that responsibility forget that responsibility history has always shown the same outcome The people remember who stood with them and who stood against them John Adams warned us warned the nation about the dangers of power when he wrote "There's danger from all men The only maximum of a free government ought to be to trust no man living with power to endanger the public liberty." The founders designed our system with this understanding Power must always answer to the people Wyoming has always had a frontier spirit Not the lawlessness of the frontier but the independence of it The belief that communities should govern themselves that citizens should be heard and that government exists to serve the people not manage them That belief is not outdated It is the foundation of our republic Thomas Jefferson put it plainly "When the people fear the government there's tyranny When the government fears the people there's liberty." Which brings me to the words that helped ignite the spirit of the American independence In 1775 Patrick Henry wrote before the Virginia Convention and

declared "Give me liberty or give me death." And those words were not a threat They were a declaration of principle Dakota thank you Yes ma'am You might have to pick that microphone up and hold it because it might be too low for you Just work Uh my name is uh Johnny Nicely My name is Johnny Nicely and I have some simple words to say I hope that one of these speeches that these people have told you will sway one of your hearts and minds to say no to annexation Thank you Nice job Johnny Jennifer Uh Jordan Weisdorf if you can unmute yourself Thank you Jordan Wisdorf can you guys hear me Okay we can Thank

